📗
  📊 What a test can measure: how much the program has allocated, and how much
  of that serving a request left behind.

    📈🐇📊 app 🔤GET /todos HTTP/1.1❌r❌nHost: test❌r❌n❌r❌n🔤 500 2000❗️ ➡️ grown

  The compiler and the standard library leak in ways that are easy to trip
  over (the repository's README lists them), and a handler that leaks on
  every request ends its server sooner or later. This is how an application
  finds out before its users do: play each kind of request a few thousand
  times and see that nothing stayed.

  The count is the allocator's, and it takes a freed block that sits in a
  thread's cache for one in use. Run the test program with
  GLIBC_TUNABLES=glibc.malloc.tcache_count=0 and a handler that keeps
  nothing measures exactly 0; without it, allow a few kilobytes.
